 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**For a short trip outside today (Saturday, October 17), you generally don’t need a mask for the haze.** Singapore’s 1-hour PM2.5 readings were in the *Elevated* range at 7 a.m.; the Ministry of Health says an N95 or equivalent is not needed for a short commute. Do take it easy outdoors: the 24-hour air-quality reading was *Unhealthy* across Singapore at 8 a.m. ([channelnewsasia.com](https://www.channelnewsasia.com/singapore/singapore-haze-psi-pm25-oct-17-6466816))\n\n**Check the latest 1-hour PM2.5 reading in myENV or on haze.gov.sg just before you go**, because conditions can change. If it reaches *High*, people who are elderly, pregnant, or have heart or lung disease should avoid going outdoors; if they must go, a well-fitting N95 or equivalent can reduce exposure. At *Very High* levels, healthy people should also minimise outdoor activity and may use one if going out is necessary. ([moh.gov.sg](https://www.moh.gov.sg/others/haze/?utm_source=openai))",
